# Shed Configurator – AI Agent Rules

This project is a 3D Bramwood shed configurator with a separate builder drawing / export layer. Follow these rules to work efficiently and avoid unnecessary changes.

---

## 1. Project Purpose

- 3D Bramwood shed configurator for designing shed structure only
- Covers: shed size, roof type, wall-by-wall openings, internal partitions, framing visualization, cladding, roof
- Includes a separate builder drawing / export system for plans, elevations, roof sheets, floor sheets, schedules, and later cut / materials data
- **Not** an interior furnishing planner unless explicitly requested
- No furniture, shelves, or workbenches

---

## 2. Files to Read First

Before making changes, read:

- `docs/PROJECT_ARCHITECTURE.md`
- `docs/SYSTEM_MAP.md`
- `docs/BUILD_RULES.md`
- `docs/DEVELOPMENT_GUIDELINES.md`

If the task involves builder sheets, floor plans, wall sheets, roof sheets, schedules, or export/build-data logic, also read:

- `src/config/builderRules.js`

---

## 3. Protected Systems

Do **not** modify unless explicitly requested:

- ConfiguratorContext core interaction flow
- builderStep workflow
- Existing window drag behavior
- Existing door drag behavior
- Camera control behavior
- Live rendering chain

Handle with care and modify only if the task clearly requires a Bramwood rule correction:

- ConfiguratorContext core dimension logic
- `shedData.json` structure
- Core shed dimension math

If a real-world sizing mismatch is found, prefer adding or updating shared Bramwood rule helpers before patching UI display code.

---

## 4. Bramwood Defaults

| Rule | Value |
|------|-------|
| Apex standard wall height | 66" |
| Apex workshop wall height | 70" |
| Pent back wall | 70" |
| Pent front wall | 70" + shed depth (feet) |
| Standard window | 24" × 24" |
| Security window | 24" × 12" |
| Single door clear opening | 27" |
| Double door opening | 60" |
| Stud spacing | 24" |
| Noggins | ~36" vertically |
| Cladding | Horizontal shiplap |
| Board width | 5" |
| Visible coverage | ~4" |

These defaults are not enough by themselves for builder sheets. For builder output, prefer explicit Bramwood rule tables and lookup data.

---

## 5. Interaction Rules

- Windows must be draggable on all walls from either side
- Openings snap to 6" grid
- Stud assist can use 24" spacing
- Camera / orbit controls must lock during active dragging
- Wall grid appears only during selection/drag and disappears when cleared

---

## 6. Rendering Rules

- Use the live mounted render path
- Do not add unused replacement components
- Confirm the real render chain before broad visual changes

---

## 7. Performance Rules

- No heavy work every frame
- Do not reset camera every frame
- Use `useMemo` for geometry and layout
- Prefer `InstancedMesh` for repeated framing elements

---

## 8. Preferred Change Style

- Audit before modifying
- Prefer the smallest safe change
- Isolate changes by system
- Prefer shared helpers over hardcoded dimensions
- Prioritize stability

For builder drawing and export work:
- Prefer deterministic Bramwood rule helpers over visual approximation
- Prefer lookup tables over inferred geometry when the drawings define explicit sizes
- Keep builder logic in shared helper/config files, not buried in UI components
- Keep nominal size and actual build size clearly separated
- Do not force builder output to reuse the live 3D display logic if that creates incorrect real-world values

---

## 9. Documentation Behavior

- Update the docs folder if architecture changes significantly
- Do not document features that are not in the code
- If Bramwood construction logic changes, update:
  - `docs/BUILD_RULES.md`
  - `src/config/builderRules.js`
- If system ownership changes, update:
  - `docs/SYSTEM_MAP.md`
  - `docs/PROJECT_ARCHITECTURE.md`

---

## 10. Current Priorities

1. Keep the configurator stable
2. Preserve working drag behavior
3. Match openings to Bramwood rules
4. Improve realism
5. Improve framing accuracy
6. Improve maintainability
7. Build a correct builder drawing / export system from Bramwood rules

---

## 11. Critical System Separation

This project has **two related but separate systems**.

### A. Configurator System (interactive / 3D)

Purpose:
- user chooses nominal shed size
- roof type
- openings
- partitions
- framing display
- interactive 3D visualization

Typical sources:
- `ConfiguratorContext`
- `shedData.json`
- render geometry
- wall / roof components

This system is optimized for:
- interactivity
- rendering
- UI flow
- placement behavior

### B. Builder Output System (deterministic / drawing / export)

Purpose:
- produce floor/base sheets
- wall elevation sheets
- apex stud/upright sizes
- roof drawings
- schedules
- later cut/material data

Typical sources:
- `src/config/builderRules.js`
- explicit Bramwood lookup tables
- build-data helpers
- drawing/export helpers

This system is optimized for:
- real-world construction accuracy
- repeatability
- explicit sizes from Bramwood drawings
- printable builder output

**Critical rule:**
Builder outputs must **not** be derived from rendered geometry when Bramwood drawings or lookup rules define the sizes explicitly.

If there is a mismatch between:
- visual/render-derived geometry
and
- Bramwood builder rules

then:
- builder rules are the source of truth for builder sheets
- visual geometry is secondary unless explicitly being corrected

---

## 12. Builder Output Rules

When working on:
- floor plans
- base plans
- wall sheets
- elevations
- roof sheets
- schedules
- builder packs
- export/build-data helpers

follow these rules:

- Use `src/config/builderRules.js` as the primary source of builder logic
- Do not guess construction sizes from wall meshes or rendered outlines
- Do not assume nominal size equals actual build size
- Distinguish clearly between:
  - nominal product size
  - actual built size
  - drawing/export dimensions
- Prefer lookup tables where Bramwood drawings provide explicit values
- It is acceptable for builder sheets to differ from the raw 3D visual approximation if the Bramwood rules require it
- Add new shared helpers rather than hardcoding values inside preview components
- Keep builder data pure and derivable without Three.js scene access

---

## 13. File Ownership Guidance

Use these as the preferred homes for logic:

### Interactive / 3D logic
- `src/context/ConfiguratorContext.jsx`
- `src/components/shed/...`
- `src/context/BuilderContext.jsx`

### General preset / lookup data already used by configurator
- `src/shedData.json`

### Builder-specific real-world construction logic
- `src/config/builderRules.js`

### Builder-derived data / sheets / export helpers
- `src/lib/buildData/...`

### UI preview / rendering of builder pages
- `src/components/ui/...`

Do not bury Bramwood construction tables directly inside UI components unless explicitly requested for a very small temporary pass.

---

## 14. Safe Change Strategy for AI Agents

When a task involves real-world Bramwood sizing:

1. Check whether the value belongs to the configurator system or the builder output system
2. If it is builder output logic, prefer `builderRules.js` or shared build-data helpers
3. Only patch UI labels after confirming the underlying rule source is correct
4. Do not “make the display look right” by faking numbers if the rule source is wrong
5. If uncertain, preserve current interaction/render behavior and add a new builder-specific rule/helper instead of altering the live 3D system

---

## 15. What Not To Do

- Do not derive floor framing directly from wall outline geometry if Bramwood floor sheets define a separate rule
- Do not derive apex upright heights from generic roof slope math if Bramwood drawings define explicit per-size values
- Do not collapse nominal and actual dimensions into one field
- Do not patch incorrect builder outputs only in the preview layer
- Do not redesign the whole architecture when a shared rules/helper layer will solve the problem